'''Balisca-Soviet Union relations''' ({{lang-bl|Relacions Balisca-Unión Sovietica}}; {{Langx|ru|Отношения Балиски и Советского Союза|Otnosheniya Baliski i Sovetskogo Soyuza}}) refers to [[W:Bilateralism|bilateral relations]] between [[Balisca]] and the [[Soviet Union]]. Relations between the two nations have fluctuated greatly throughout their history, but the two countries are considered to be each other's closest allies, with relations categorized as 'unlimited' and cooperative.

Balisca was the first in the [[W:Americas|Americas]] and one of the first in the world to recognize the Soviet Union, doing so on 20 September 1923 amid a wave of revolutionary optimism. Earlier, it vocally opposed the [[W:Allied intervention in the Russian Civil War|Allied intervention in the Russian Civil War]]. Relations cooled considerably with the consolidation of power by the [[W:Bolsheviks|Bolsheviks]], and remained distant until the Soviet entry into the [[W:Second World War|Second World War]], after which the rapidly mobilizing Balisca provided significant material support. During the Cold War, relations between both countries continued to improve and were further solidified by the USSR's support of Balisca during the [[South Atlantic War]], ultimately leading to Balisca abandoning it's policy of [[W:Armed neutrality|armed neutrality]] through the [[Mutual Arms and Technology Agreement]]. Following the [[Soviet Reformation]] relations have deepened immensely. Economic interdependence has increased since the Soviet Union joined the [[Concepción Organization of Co-Operation]] in 1994, as the bloc seeks to build an alternative to the United States-lead [[W:Liberal international order|neoliberal international order]].

The two nations cooperate extensively in several spheres, including the development of civil technology, [[Mutual Arms and Technology Agreement|arms development]], nuclear energy technology, [[Project Luna Horizons|space exploration]], education, telecommunications and cybersecurity.

Baliscan citizens are known to have a generally positive view towards the Soviet Union, with 76% of Baliscans viewing the Soviets favorably and positively as of 2019, making it one of the most Soviet-friendly countries in the Americas. Similarly, over 74% of Soviet citizens have a positive view of Balisca, and the Soviet government generally regards Balisca as a key international partner vital to its global interests. The deepening alignment of Balisca and the Soviet Union has [[Balisca-United States relations#21st Century|increasingly prompted concern]] in the United States.

